```

Here `p` is advanced; in the C++ outer loop `p` is a Node* and after the merge, `p = q`. In my Rust version I use `pu` for the head of the p-run and `p` (Option) as the iteration variable. Let me re-check my port:

```rust
while let Some(pu) = p {
    ...
    let mut q = Some(pu);
    for _ in 0..in_size { p_size += 1; q = next_z(q); if none break; }
    let mut q_size = in_size;
    let mut pu = pu;  // shadows
    while p_size > 0 || (q_size > 0 && q.is_some()) {
        if p_size == 0 { e = q; advance q; }
        else if q_size == 0 || q.is_none() { e = pu; advance p (and pu); }
        else if z(pu) <= z(q) { e = pu; advance; }
        else { e = q; advance q; }
    }
    p = q;
}
```

In C++, `p` and `q` are the cursors; the outer loop's `p` initially is the run head. When we take from the p-side, `p = p->nextZ`. At the end of merging, `p = q` (whatever remains). In my port, taking from p-side sets `p = self.nodes[pu].next_z` and `pu = p.unwrap_or(pu)`. Hmm — if p becomes None but p_size > 0 still (shouldn't happen because p-run has p_size nodes all valid)... Actually in C++, p always points to valid nodes within the p-run; p_size counts down as we consume. After consuming all p_size nodes, p may point into q-run territory or null — but then we set p = q at loop end anyway. My shadowing with pu: when p_side taken, I set p = next_z(pu) and pu = that (or keep pu if None — bug? If next_z is None but p_size > 0, that would be a bug, but the p-run was counted: p_size nodes exist from pu, so advancing p_size-1 times... wait.

Let's trace: q starts at pu; we advance q p_size times counting (up to in_size), breaking if q becomes None. So the p-run has p_size nodes: pu, ..., and the node before q. Cursors: p-side cursor initially pu; each take: e = cursor; cursor = cursor->nextZ; pSize--. After consuming all pSize nodes, cursor points at q (or null). In C++, `p` itself is the cursor, and at merge end `p = q`. My pu advances as `pu = p.unwrap_or(pu)` where p = next_z(pu). If next_z(pu) is None while p_size > 0 — can't happen since p-run has p_size nodes. If p_size reaches 0, pu won't be used again (first branch requires p_size == 0, so pu unused). OK, equivalent. Then `p = q` at the end. Fine.

But one subtlety: in the p-take branches I wrote `p = self.nodes[pu].next_z;` — that's right.

Also `self.nodes[tail.unwrap()].next_z = None;` — tail is Some after at least one merge (list nonempty). OK.

